  "The Quiet Shore": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — pale coast; shoreline of fine pale sand; a soundless, perfectly still water-horizon behaving like an unbreathing sea, and pale-flowered dune-grass; moon-white sand that never shifts, salt-grass chimes that ring once and never twice. The far western edge of the Demi-Plane — a coast without a sea. The ground slopes to a shoreline of fine pale sand, and beyond stretches a horizon of flat, still air that behaves like water without being water. The Chamberlain calls it the Quiet Shore: no waves, no wind, no carried sound. The most peaceful place in the dimension and the loneliest. The Demi-Plane has not yet decided whether this will be sea, desert, or something else entirely.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 8–18): glass crabs skimming the unbreathing horizon, pale shore spirits combing the tideline without footsteps, dune foxes denning behind the salt-grass, and — near the boundary itself — rare luminous fish-like entities gliding beneath the not-water. All gentle; all silent.\n\nResources: moon-sand scooped unbroken from the tideline, saltgrass with its faint chime, shell crystal, and pale driftwood that arrives from a sea that does not exist.\n\nKnown Locations: the Stillwater Shore — the region's formal coast where land meets the unbreathing horizon — and the Moonwhite Strand, the long white dune line backing it. Everything else stays unmarked and half-mysterious: hidden tidal caves that open in the not-water's edge, objects washed ashore from nowhere, and impossible reflections that stand or move in ways the real world cannot account for — found occasionally, never beforehand.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The flat horizon is the western boundary, expressed differently from the Veiled Edge — subtle and still, detectable only by the absence of detail. A willed sea would recede the boundary westward; a willed desert would solidify it. Until then, it waits.",
