  "The Grey Boundary": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — fog-wall moor; cool damp moorland ending in a wall of grey fog; within the fog, shapes flicker too briefly to name. The southern boundary of the Demi-Plane — a wall of grey fog stretching horizon to horizon. Cool, damp, opaque. Walking into it feels like walking into nothing: no resistance, no ground change, just a growing sense that forward is not a direction that exists here. The firmest edge of the dimension — it does not expand here, and the fog does not retreat. Whatever the creator wills, this border holds.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 22–30): fog shades drifting along the fog wall, boundary wisps tracing the dimension's edge, grey moths in slow circling flights, and rarely an anchor guardian — a silent standing watch-thing that appears only when something tests the boundary. Most wildlife keeps to the fog's margin.\n\nResources: anchor-stone (the dimension's most stable mineral), fog crystal, boundary moss, and condensed dimensional mist gathered at the wall's edge.\n\nKnown Locations: The Grey Fog Wall — the boundary itself, the region's defining landmark; and the Anchor Cairn, the Chamberlain's nearest marker stone. Everything else stays unmarked: older forgotten cairns, things half-lost inside the fog, and rare dimensional anomalies wait to be discovered rather than pointed out.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The fog is fixed because it anchors the Demi-Plane to a stable point in the dimensional void. Unlike the other shifting boundaries, the southern border must remain constant — if it moved, the dimension's position relative to the mortal world would drift and the Demi-Plane Gate could become unpredictable. The Chamberlain knows no technical reason, only an instinct that this border must not be touched.",
