  "The Fade": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — gentle hills dissolving into featureless mist; things that enter the mist leave their sound behind first, then their color. The southeast boundary — where the Rift March's dramatic terrain softens into gentle hills that gradually fade into featureless mist. The transition is almost imperceptible: solid ground among rolling hills, then fog with no sense of direction. Not dangerous — it simply ends. The Demi-Plane extends no further. The Chamberlain has marked the boundary with small cairns at the exact points where ground stops being ground.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 20–30): mistlings rolling through the low fog, pale walkers striding the far hills, echo spirits repeating the movements of the living a breath behind, and — rarely — fade-beasts, upper-level things that graze on certainty itself. Most encounters here are glimpses; few hold.\n\nResources: fadeglass (thin panes of half-real material), mist pearls formed where fog pools overnight, soundless stone cut from the grey hills, and boundary herbs that grow only within a stone's throw of the last cairn.\n\nKnown Locations: the Cairn Line — the Chamberlain's row of boundary stones where ground stops being ground; and The Dissolving Hills, the fading upland itself. Everything deeper stays unmarked: hidden paths that exist only while walked, vanished structures surfacing briefly through the fog, and temporary landscapes that appear and disappear overnight are discoveries made in the mist, not places on a map.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The Fade is the most permeable boundary — not weak, but unfinished. The dimension has not decided what it wants to be here, and fog is ambiguity made visible. Strong intention toward it thins the mist and lets new terrain begin forming. The Chamberlain watched this happen twice — both times the creator was dreaming.",
