  "Veiled Fox Sanctuary": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — sanctuary spirit-forest; mist-laced fox-spirit woodland of torii processions, a shinboku heart, and a luminous bamboo spring. The merged temperate spirit-forest and sacred shrine realm of the Demi-Plane's northwest: its woodland half is the dimension's first ecosystem — tall pale-barked trees grown from ambient potential, bioluminescent fungi, drifting foxfire motes, moss that dims underfoot to light paths, roots tapping the ambient mana flow (the Chamberlain treats it as infrastructure). Its mythic core is reserved entirely for the Hidden Estate of the Mother Fox and her private lands — the sanctum of Tsukiko Kuzunoha, creator of this dimension. Entered only by her will, the Demi-Plane Gate ability, or the Demi-Plane Anchor item; no road, gate, spell, or teleportation opens a path without her permission. The atmosphere is unmistakably otherwhere: vermilion torii gates standing in procession, stone fox statues wearing red bibs and gazing from mossy pedestals, paper-shide streamers fluttering from sacred ropes, and lanterns whose flames burn pale violet like dissociated foxfire. A still shinboku tree stands at the sanctuary's heart ringed by a rope of braided gold and white paper — a kami's tree in the oldest tradition. Pine, maple, shide, kōyō, and sakaki grow in deliberate arrangement; a spring of clear water pools beneath sweeping bamboo, and stone paths cross in shimenawa-bound silence — inspired by a Japan that predates maps: something between a kami's inner shrine, an aristocrat's hidden garden, and a fox spirit's dreamed estate. The land is elegant, quiet, and slightly unreal — sunbeams slant at angles that do not quite match the hour, and the light tastes faintly of cedar smoke. Visitors attend only if bidden. The Hidden Estate of the Mother Fox sits here, its true grounds nestling in this sanctuary rather than in the basin, and the estate's fox-spirit staff pass freely between them. Repository of fungal and botanical resources for those welcomed inside.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 12–25 for outsiders; effectively safe for Tsukiko and permitted residents): kitsune spirits moving between lantern paths, shrine guardians walking scheduled perimeters, foxfire wisps tending the ritual flames, sacred birds and spirit deer that announce rather than flee from strangers. Intruders who enter uninvited find the land itself uncooperative — paths lengthen, lanterns dim, feet slow.\n\nResources: foxfire blooms, sacred bamboo, rare bioluminescent fungi, shrine herbs, and spirit wood from fallen pale-barked branches — all available to permitted residents of the sanctuary and the estate staff who tend them.\n\nKnown Locations: the Hidden Estate of the Mother Fox — the region's one established estate marker — plus the Shinboku, the Bamboo Spring, and the Torii Procession along the inner shrine paths. Everything deeper stays veiled: secret gardens, spirit dwellings, hidden shrines, and the estate's true grounds reveal themselves only while exploring — or with Tsukiko's permission, which remains the lawful and wise way in.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "No NPC may enter without Tsukiko's direct, spoken invitation — not the Chamberlain, not party members, not allied spirits, not outside creatures of any kind. The sanctuary's foxfire lanterns are not decoration: they are wards that read intent. Anyone crossing with false or thieving purpose feels their feet slow and their mind fog, as though walking into deep water with no shore visible. The shinboku at the heart of the sanctuary feeds on Tsukiko's presence — aether drawn from her divinity seeps into the roots and pools beneath the estate as a reserve she can call upon. The woodland half fills that ecological role itself: its roots tap the ambient mana flow, filtering and distributing it through the soil; destroying that forest would make mana distribution uneven across the western regions. The Chamberlain treats the whole sanctuary-forest as infrastructure, not decoration. Since the sanctuary's woodland deepened, its winds have carried faint fox-shaped shadows and incense — the Chamberlain suspects the sanctuary's spirit-folk walk to the First Meadow's edge through the trees. He has proposed, three times, that the Demi-Plane's entire mana infrastructure could route through the sanctuary's shinboku — each proposal was politely declined, and each time the Chamberlain presumed the creator had reasons she did not wish to share.",
